Binary Tree Creation and Traversal in C++

This repository contains two C++ programs demonstrating dynamic binary tree construction and recursive depth-first traversals.

Program 1: Binary Tree Creation
Defines a TreeNode class with an integer value and left and right pointers. The CreateTree function recursively builds the binary tree by taking user input for the current node value and asking whether left or right children exist (1 for YES, 0 for NO).

Sample Output for Program 1 (for a tree with root 1, left child 2, and right child 3)
Enter Value of Node : 1
If left Exist ? 0 for NO and 1 for YES1
Enter Value of Node : 2
If left Exist ? 0 for NO and 1 for YES0
If right Exist ? 0 for NO and 1 for YES0
If right Exist ? 0 for NO and 1 for YES1
Enter Value of Node : 3
If left Exist ? 0 for NO and 1 for YES0
If right Exist ? 0 for NO and 1 for YES0

Program 2: Binary Tree Creation and Traversal
Extends the first program by adding three recursive traversal functions after constructing the tree. Preorder visits Root, Left, Right. Inorder visits Left, Root, Right. Postorder visits Left, Right, Root.

Sample Output for Program 2 (for a tree with root 1, left child 2, and right child 3)
Enter Value of Node : 1
If left Exist ? 0 for NO and 1 for YES1
Enter Value of Node : 2
If left Exist ? 0 for NO and 1 for YES0
If right Exist ? 0 for NO and 1 for YES0
If right Exist ? 0 for NO and 1 for YES1
Enter Value of Node : 3
If left Exist ? 0 for NO and 1 for YES0
If right Exist ? 0 for NO and 1 for YES0
Preorder: 1 2 3
Inorder: 2 1 3
Postorder: 2 3 1

Time and Space Complexity
Time Complexity: O(N) for both tree creation and all three traversals, where N is the total number of nodes in the tree, because every node is visited exactly once.
Space Complexity: O(H) auxiliary space for the recursion call stack, where H is the height of the tree. In the worst case of a skewed tree, this is O(N), and in the best case of a balanced tree, this is O(log N).
